From: Igni <igni.li@quectel.com>
Subject: [PATCH] pw-pal-plugin: give the one PAL handle to the selected default sink

0007 hands the one deep-buffer handle of a direction to whichever instance
reaches STREAMING first, and that is not necessarily the sink the user is
listening to. Every pal_sink_* node is held in the running state by whoever
records its monitor, and the GNOME sound picker records all six at once:
measured, six links pal_sink_speaker -> gnome-control-center, and node 44 went
back to "suspended" the moment that client was killed. So all six instances ask
for the handle together, the first one scheduled takes it, and the others retry
against a slot the winner has no reason to release. Measured: the speaker held
it while the headset sink -- the default, with the player's stream open on it --
burned all 90 attempts in 3 min and stayed silent with its node, its links and
its entry in the picker all showing as healthy.

Follow default.audio.sink / default.audio.source instead: only the selected
instance may open a handle, the others close theirs as soon as the selection
moves, and the newly selected one is started from the selection callback.
pal-jack-default writes those keys on a jack event and the shell writes them on
a manual switch, so a handover is that metadata change plus one graph open, in
either direction.

A start that comes back -EAGAIN is that handover in flight -- a direction-mate
still holds the handle -- and not a failure: keep the timer armed and reset the
budget, otherwise the instance that is about to be selected gives up on it.

Checked: series 0001-0008 applies in bitbake's order and the patched source
compiles with 0 errors.

Signed-off-by: Igni <igni.li@quectel.com>
---
